{
  "abstract": "We prove that directed strongly regular graphs with parameters $(86,38,37,16,17)$ and $(86,47,46,25,26)$ do not exist, settling a complementary pair marked open in the Hobart–Brouwer table. The proof uses the permutation formed by the unique outgoing and incoming nonreciprocal arcs. Chord alternation, an equitable quotient on the kernel of its skew part, and exact primitive-root tests reduce all 1,146 possible cycle partitions to seventeen types. A short congruence argument excludes the five types with exactly two 4-cycles. For five 4-cycles, a Gaussian Gram corner has 57 normalized possibilities, independently recovered from 625 ungauged corners. Determinants exclude four types; in the remaining square case the forced completion yields compatibility graphs of maximum clique size three, although five rows are required. The other seven types are treated in an eighth-root sector. Four fail a congruence and two fail a norm bound. In the last mixed 24/8-cycle type, all 4,624 small corners reduce to 64 with a scalar Gram matrix. None of 819,200 forced diagonal blocks is admissible. A standalone exact checker regenerates the binary alphabets and verifies the full finite obstruction; independent implementations and genuine graph or planted algebraic controls check the principal reductions. The supplement reproduces the complete proof with standard-library Python and C++17. No automorphism assumption, incomplete search, or floating-point exclusion is used.",
